## ⚡ Step-by-Step Flow: Post-Install Hardening Workflow


1. Mount ISO and install Windows Server 2022 (Standard Desktop Experience)
2. Resolve virtual hardware and installer conflicts (Floppy / Unattended Setup)
3. Set initial secure local Administrator account password
4. Rename random computer name (e.g., WIN-X98A1) to standardized hostname (DC01)
5. Assign static IP parameters (192.168.1.10/24, Gateway: 192.168.1.1)
6. Configure DNS address to loopback (127.0.0.1) for future AD integration
7. Enable Remote Desktop (RDP) and adjust firewall rules
8. Reboot server to finalize changes and verify network stack


---

## 🛠️ Execution Methods

### Option A: PowerShell Automation (Enterprise Deployment)

# 1. Rename Host to Enterprise Standard
Rename-Computer -NewName "DC01" -Force

# 2. View Available Network Adapter Alias
Get-NetAdapter

# 3. Assign Static IP & Gateway
New-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -IPAddress 192.168.1.10 -PrefixLength 24 -DefaultGateway 192.168.1.1

# 4. Configure DNS to Local Loopback
Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -ServerAddresses ("127.0.0.1")

# 5. Enable Remote Desktop & Firewall Exceptions
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server' -Name "fDenyTSConnections" -Value 0
Enable-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup "Remote Desktop"

### Option B: Interactive Utility Method (sconfig)

1. Open Command Prompt or PowerShell.
2. Type sconfig and press Enter.
3. Select Option 2 (Computer Name) -> Set to DC01.
4. Select Option 8 (Network Settings) -> Select Adapter -> Assign Static IP, Subnet Mask, Gateway, and DNS.
5. Select Option 7 (Remote Desktop) -> Choose E (Enable).
6. Select Option 13 (Restart Server) to commit changes.

